Code Design:

  Each business feature is a package with the same 4-layer recipe inside:

  feature/
  ├── api/          → Controllers (HTTP in) + dto/ (request/response shapes)
  ├── service/      → Business logic (the "what happens" rules)
  ├── domain/       → Entities & value objects (the data + invariants)
  └── persistence/  → Spring Data repositories (how data is saved/loaded)

--------------------
why we create our entity on persistence?
1. JPA forces you to weaken your class
2. Business tests stay fast
3. Storage details don't leak into business logic
4. You can swap storage without touching rules

---------------------
request Journey:

  1. HTTP arrives
     ↓
  2. JwtAuthenticationFilter (security/)        ← reads Authorization header,
                                                   verifies signature, builds AuthenticatedUser,
                                                   puts it in SecurityContext
     ↓
  3. Spring Security checks role                ← "/api/v1/owner/**" requires ROLE_OWNER
                                                   (SecurityConfig.java:39)
     ↓
  4. OwnerInvoiceController.markPaid()          ← extracts invoiceId, calls service
     ↓
  5. InvoiceService.markPaid()                  ← THE business rules live here:
                                                   load invoice → check it belongs to this
                                                   owner → check status transition is legal
                                                   → change status → save
     ↓
  6. InvoiceRepository (persistence/)           ← Spring Data: you write only the interface,
                                                   Spring generates the SQL queries
     ↓
  7. PostgreSQL                                  ← schema managed by Flyway migrations
                                                   (resources/db/migration/V*.sql)
     ↓
  8. Response bubbles back up wrapped in ApiResponse  ← your standard JSON envelope
